@yolk_vat-y/dsh-project-memory README · Toggling features：- `roots.allow` — allow a directory even when it is on the built-in list, matched exactly. The filesystem root is not eligible.
- `roots.deny` — this directory cannot be a root, matched exactly; its subdirectories can still be roots of their own.
- `roots.exclude` — no file in this subtree enters the index, matched by path-boundary prefix (subdirectories included); no new root is inferred inside it either. A complete scan of a parent root skips it and removes entries already indexed under it.

@yolk_vat-y/dsh-project-memory README · Reproduce it on your own project：The measurement ships with the repository **and with the published npm package** (`scripts/` is part of the tarball). It needs **no dsh instance, no network and no model calls**, and never touches your project's own store — results go to a temp directory and are removed afterwards:

```bash
npm run bench -- /path/to/your/project

@yolk_vat-y/dsh-project-memory README · or, with options:：node scripts/bench.mjs /path/to/your/project [--json] [--samples 100] [--no-pdf] [--keep]
```

It reports the cold index split into read+hash / extract / commit, cold load, IDF rebuild, cold and hot query latency (p50/p95/max over 100 sampled queries through the shipped scorer), single-file hot re-index, store size, resident heap and bytes per entry. Example — the frontend app row above:

@yolk_vat-y/dsh-project-memory README · Design tradeoffs：- **A short filesystem lock, not an embedded database** — each save holds an exclusive `.write.lock` only for the read-modify-write (parsing stays outside it) and waits synchronously for up to 15 s for a busy peer (correctness comes from acquiring the lock, not from background retries); a write that still cannot land is registered for a synchronous flush when the process exits or the plugin is disposed, **without interrupting the user** — memory writes are idempotent and cheap to redo; set `DSH_PROJECT_MEMORY_DEBUG=1` for a one-line diagnostic. The lock carries `pid` / `host` / `token`: a live same-host holder is never preempted, release verifies ownership, and a crashed same-host lock is reclaimed immediately. Two dsh instances can share one store. No WAL, no daemon, no native dependency.
- **Watch: compute outside, commit inside** — no lock is held during parsing, and polling with mtime + content hash is used instead of `fs.watch`: it behaves identically on network drives, Docker volumes and WSL, without duplicate-trigger or missed-event failure modes.
